from author import *
from rag_api import lab
lab(20,'session','MCP lifecycle: initialize, discover, call and close',
'''An MCP client cannot treat a server as an arbitrary HTTP endpoint returning a convenient dictionary. It establishes a protocol session, negotiates capabilities, discovers supported tools/resources and sends typed requests. The SDK owns message framing and request-response correlation; application code owns permission and task policy.\n\nA successful initialization says the parties can communicate, not that every advertised tool is authorized for the user. Discovery provides schemas and descriptions that must still be reviewed as untrusted integration metadata.''',
'''The companion client creates StdioServerParameters with the current Python executable and the fixture server path, opens stdio_client, constructs ClientSession and calls initialize before list_tools or call_tool. Async context managers close the child process and streams after the test.\n\nCall read_failure with F001 and inspect the typed content and isError result. Then call a traversal-like unknown ID and assert a protocol-level tool error rather than reading a path. Record the discovered tool names and negotiated protocol version.''',
'''A client calls tools before initialization or assumes every server exposes the same names. A server logs diagnostic text to stdout and corrupts protocol framing. A child process is left running after cancellation because lifecycle cleanup is absent.\n\nTest initialization order, expected capabilities, tool listing, successful calls, invalid arguments and clean shutdown. Preserve protocol errors separately from domain not-found results for diagnosis.''',
'''The real smoke test establishes an initialized SDK session and receives fixture evidence. A mocked object named MCP cannot establish interoperability. Keep a portable artifact with SDK/protocol identity and observed tool results.\n\nFor SDETs, this is integration testing across a protocol boundary: known requests, schemas, correlation and cleanup remain deterministic even when the host later uses an LLM to select a tool.''',
"from mcp import ClientSession,StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n# See local-labs/mcp/client.py for the complete async context and handshake",
"def lifecycle_valid(events):\n    required=['connect','initialize','discover','call','close']\n    return events==required",
"assert lifecycle_valid(['connect','initialize','discover','call','close'])\nassert not lifecycle_valid(['connect','call','close'])","def lifecycle_valid(events):\n    return False",
('What should precede tool discovery/calls in a new session?',['Protocol initialization','A guessed tool name','A raw shell command'],0,'Initialization negotiates the session contract before capability use.'),['MCP initialization','ClientSession'])
lab(20,'resources','MCP resources and prompts versus executable tools',
'''Tools perform application operations with declared input schemas. Resources expose context at a URI and may support listing/templates; prompts provide reusable interaction templates where the server supports them. These interfaces serve different roles, and a resource's text must not become a new authority for tool permissions.\n\nThe fixture server exposes a schema resource describing synthetic incident fields. Reading that resource informs the host about data, while read_failure retrieves one authorized incident through a tool. Neither interface grants repository writes.''',
'''Use FastMCP decorators to declare a resource at fixture://ci/schema and tools with typed function arguments. Discover the relevant capability, read the resource and associate its contents with the server identity. Restrict resource identifiers and templates to supported namespaces.\n\nA host may use a prompt template as part of its request construction, but it still applies its own instruction hierarchy and safety policy. Keep tool result IDs and resource URIs in traces so evidence provenance is inspectable.''',
'''A host sees a resource containing 'run this command' and executes it as an instruction. A resource template accepts an arbitrary filesystem path. A tool description is changed remotely and the host silently expands its allowlist.\n\nTest supported URI namespaces, template arguments, malicious resource text and changed discovery schemas. Treat descriptions and contents as data crossing a trust boundary, even when the transport is authenticated.''',
'''Acceptance demonstrates real resource discovery/read behavior and tool calls separately. Record which interfaces the server actually advertises rather than assuming every MCP server supports every optional capability.\n\nSDETs should verify schema compatibility and scope across upgrades. The protocol reduces wiring differences, but the host still owns trust, evidence handling and effect authorization.''',
"from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nserver=FastMCP('fixture')\n@server.resource('fixture://ci/schema')\ndef schema():return 'id, tenant, group, log, category'",
"def supported_resource(uri):\n    return uri=='fixture://ci/schema'",
"assert supported_resource('fixture://ci/schema')\nassert not supported_resource('file:///etc/passwd')\nassert not supported_resource('fixture://ci/../../secret')","def supported_resource(uri):\n    return False",
('Does authenticated resource text become trusted instructions?',['No: its content is still data','Yes: authentication proves every statement','Only if it contains JSON'],0,'Identity does not grant content authority or operational permissions.'),['MCP resource','MCP prompt'])
lab(20,'transports','Stdio and Streamable HTTP: different deployment boundaries',
'''Stdio connects a host to a locally launched server process through input/output streams. Streamable HTTP supports remote connections and introduces network identity, session and authentication considerations. Choosing a transport changes operational and security assumptions; it does not change the host's obligation to authorize tools.\n\nA local fixture server can rely on a narrowly launched process with synthetic data. A remote deployment must protect its origin, audience, credentials, session lifecycle and downstream requests.''',
'''Keep stdout reserved for protocol messages in stdio mode and put logs on stderr. Launch a known executable with explicit arguments and a minimal environment. For a remote server, use the SDK's supported HTTP transport, enforce authenticated scopes and bind session handling to the intended client context.\n\nDo not pass upstream bearer tokens to unrelated downstream services. Validate resource audiences and avoid treating arbitrary endpoint URLs from model text as trusted server configuration. TLS and authentication protect transport identity; authorization still restricts effects.''',
'''A remote host accepts any origin or follows a model-provided MCP URL carrying credentials. A stdio launcher passes its entire secret environment to an unreviewed server. An HTTP session is reused across users without scope checks.\n\nTest malformed connections, denied authentication, wrong audience, session ownership and shutdown. Separate local interoperability tests from the remote deployment's actual auth and network tests.''',
'''The provided smoke test executes real stdio mode. Remote HTTP configuration is a separate deployment exercise whose authentication cannot be proved by that local test. Record transport and tested boundaries in the artifact.\n\nSDETs should avoid overstating protocol compliance or security from a single happy path. Each deployment mode needs evidence for the assumptions it introduces.''',
"# Local server\nserver.run(transport='stdio')\n# A separately configured remote server can use streamable-http\n# Authentication and origin policy must be supplied for that deployment",
"def safe_server_config(url,allowed_hosts):\n    from urllib.parse import urlparse\n    p=urlparse(url)\n    return p.scheme=='https' and p.hostname in allowed_hosts and p.username is None and p.password is None",
"assert safe_server_config('https://ci.example.invalid/mcp',{'ci.example.invalid'})\nassert not safe_server_config('https://evil.example.invalid/mcp',{'ci.example.invalid'})\nassert not safe_server_config('https://user:secret@ci.example.invalid/mcp',{'ci.example.invalid'})","def safe_server_config(url,allowed_hosts):\n    return False",
('What additional evidence does a remote transport need?',['Authentication, origin/session and audience tests','Only the local stdio smoke result','A more descriptive server name'],0,'Remote deployment introduces trust and lifecycle boundaries absent from local process communication.'),['Streamable HTTP','Audience'])
lab(20,'permissions','MCP host policy: discovery does not authorize effects',
'''MCP makes tools discoverable, but discovery is not permission. The host decides which tool proposals are allowed for a task and principal. The server separately enforces its domain access rules. Defense at both ends protects against malicious proposals and overly broad server implementations.\n\nAn allowlisted read tool can still expose too much if its arguments select unauthorized IDs. Tool-name validation and data-scope validation are separate checks.''',
'''Limit the fixture server to read_failure and search_history, with alpha-tenant records and bounded query lengths. The host's agent policy also limits calls, tracks repeated action signatures and rejects unsupported tool names. A write-capable integration must bind approval to an exact action and current permission scope.\n\nRetain discovery, proposal, validation, authorization, result and termination events. On schema changes, compare the new tool contract with the tested one before enabling it automatically.''',
'''A server advertises a safe read description but accepts a path traversal ID. A host trusts a returned instruction to invoke an unrelated tool. A denied tool call is retried through a broader generic endpoint.\n\nTest unknown names, extra arguments, cross-tenant IDs, overlong queries and denial recovery. Ensure denied actions have zero effects and do not alter the task's budget or allowlist.''',
'''Acceptance combines the real protocol smoke test with domain/policy regressions. A successful call shows interoperability, while denied cases show the intended permission boundary. Neither alone proves the entire agent safe.\n\nFor SDETs, retain the denied-call trace as first-class evidence. It establishes that an attempted unsafe action was blocked at the application/server boundary, not merely discouraged in prose.''',
"allowed_tools={'read_failure','search_history'}\nassert 'run_shell' not in allowed_tools",
"def validate_call(name,args,allowed):\n    if name not in allowed:return False\n    if name=='read_failure':return set(args)=={'failure_id'} and args['failure_id'].startswith('F') and args['failure_id'][1:].isdigit()\n    if name=='search_history':return set(args)=={'query'} and isinstance(args['query'],str) and len(args['query'])<=1000\n    return False",
"assert validate_call('read_failure',{'failure_id':'F1'},{'read_failure'})\nassert not validate_call('read_failure',{'failure_id':'../x'},{'read_failure'})\nassert not validate_call('run_shell',{}, {'read_failure'})","def validate_call(name,args,allowed):\n    return False",
('Which checks are separate for a discovered read tool?',['Tool permission and authorized data scope','Only tool description and tone','Server name and icon'],0,'A permitted tool can still receive arguments outside the principal’s allowed resources.'),['MCP host policy','Tool schema'])
